==Abstract==

This NEP defines a versioned <code>signed_data</code> format for off-chain signatures in Neo N3.

==Motivation==

Without a distinct signing format, a caller could submit transaction signing data as a message and obtain a signature usable for a valid transaction. This standard separates message signatures from transaction signatures and provides a common format that can be extended to structured data.

==Specification==

The keywords MUST and MUST NOT indicate requirements as defined in RFC 2119.

===Signed data format===

The format is:

<pre>
signed_data = PREFIX || version || version_data || data
PREFIX = UTF8("Neo N3 Signed Message / NEP-21 / ")
</pre>

Here <code>||</code> denotes byte concatenation. The fixed prefix is 33 bytes, including its trailing space. <code>version</code> is one byte and determines the format of <code>version_data</code> and <code>data</code>.

===Version bytes===

{| class="wikitable"
! Version !! Meaning
|-
| <code>0x01</code> || Arbitrary byte data.
|-
| All other values || Unassigned; reserved for future formats, including structured data.
|}

Unassigned versions MUST be rejected. This NEP defines only version <code>0x01</code>.

====Version 0x01====

<code>data</code> is an arbitrary byte sequence, which may be empty. Its interpretation and encoding are outside this standard. The version-specific field is its byte length, encoded as an eight-byte unsigned little-endian integer:

<pre>
version_data = UInt64LE(byteLength(data))
signed_data = PREFIX || 0x01 || version_data || data
</pre>

The trusted signer MUST construct <code>signed_data</code> from the original data, even if it already contains a prefix. Framing MUST NOT be bypassed.

===Signing===

<pre>
signature = ECDSA_P256_SignDigest(privateKey, SHA256(signed_data))
</pre>

Apply SHA-256 exactly once before ECDSA on secp256r1 (NIST P-256). An ECDSA interface that hashes internally receives <code>signed_data</code> directly.

==Rationale==

The prefix starts with <code>0x4e</code>, which is not a valid N3 transaction version. Version <code>0x01</code> produces at least 42 bytes, distinct from the 36-byte transaction signing data. The signed version byte separates formats and allows future versions to define structured-data signing without changing this version.
